 the year because they were  going to cut the arts programming and so  he thought that was important for the  district to have wrote the check he  lives there he wanted there to be the  Arts in his community his residents led  to engagement and you see that in the  Roman world particularly as well and I  wanted to show you just this block from  Pompeii here's the entire block here and  this is one house which you can see here  just but you can see the the outline of  the walls of the house but you have this  enormous large house then you have  smaller houses apartments small shops  and all filling out the block what you  have here is a mixed-use block where  rich and poor people are living together  along with commercial establishments and  workshops and all in one space the  corollary to that that is that  engagement lead to investment in the  community is that social integration  reduces violence and we find that in the  ancient world and those communities in  antiquity where you have this mixed-use  where in fact the rich and the poor live  together work together and have this  common investment in the community  have much less urban violence  but it also means of course is that the  wealthy people like mr. Wilkes in my  community when they commit acts of  beneficence they do it in the community  so that they build bath complexes and  roads and aqueducts and so on  all right lesson 5 scale and walkability  matter and this is where I get most  directly into that sort of new urbanism  I don't mean sighs I mean scale the  scale of the community matters in terms  of not just how many people live in a  densely or not densely populated area  but what people see around them I've  been known in the past to say many nasty  things about Frank Lloyd Wright  brilliant designer his stuff sucks all  right  hail leek they're just terrible but  sorry but his notion of human scale  architecture makes sense as long as you  don't use Frank Lloyd Wright as the  scale seriously  you 