ry ones but there's also  three-story ones two stories of  residential above and the ground floor  is commercial  and so this lesson I think derives  directly from the ancient world lesson  six cities are not for everybody and  these gentlemen have done a great job  talking about gated community is talking  about what these men talk about people  moving out talk about the issue of  suburbs right and we are facing with  that today thanks to the construction of  large highways and the ability of people  to move outside commuters we have of  course enormous suburbs and I think the  pretty much the natural extension of  that and that is the gated community of  which you can see the gates of one up  there here's an example of one of these  plant communities this is one of my  personal favorites here this is in Texas  rich woods and I got to think that that  was an intentional poem  these are all very rich very wealthy  homes here and what these communities  lead to is in fact a lack of community  there is in fact a very limited range of  property values in these areas you see  things you guys have seen these driving  down the highway I expect you'll see a  sign off to the side of the road that  say houses from the 850 is to you know  990s it's a narrow range which carries  with it a meaning um the people that  you're gonna be living next to are like  you okay they're all about what you're  like okay it's comfortable it's safe  it's reassuring and there's an urban  response to this or New Urbanism  response and that is the planned  communities that we have and that is  what we might term bypass implant  urbanism so right  great term I'm learning so much here  bypass bypass misses bypass stuff  outside but implant we have as well and  you see that for example in Glenwood  Park outside well as part of Atlanta  right  Glenwood Park yeah new herbalist  community there and what's particularly  interesting about this attempt to create  a new urbanist community in this implant  